{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>A charming son is Joseph.</b> בן חן (a son of grace), and this is an Aramaic expression (Bava Metzi'a 119a): אפריון נמטיה לרבי שמען — \"the canopy is spread for Rabbi Shimon.\" \"בן פורת עלי עין\" — his charm is cast upon the eye that sees him; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 49:22). But this is very far-fetched, for expressions like these in the Talmud — Greek or Persian and other languages — have no companion in the sacred tongue. Moreover, according to what is found of it, it is nothing but an expression of blessing and praise, and the nun in it is a root. And so in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 60:13): \"And they blessed Rebekah\" — they said to her: may you be דויין ושפופין (fruitful and swarming), and they would praise only with the mouth. And it seems to me that this is from what they said (Ketuvot 54a): פורנא ליתמי — the meaning of which is the praise that they bestowed upon the orphans, and they also called the ketubbah \"פורנא\": a woman collects פורנא from them (Ketuvot 67a) — the ketubbah, which is the praise of her father's house, and as they said (Bereshit Rabbah 80:7): \"מהר\" — פראנון (they praised).\n\nBut \"בן פורת\" — either according to the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 49:22), from the language of פוריה וענפה (fruitful and branching), or according to the words of the grammarians (see Sefer haShorashim, root בנה), who made פורת from the language of \"ותארכנה פארתיו\" (Ezekiel 31:5), and they said that \"בן\" means like a sapling, and they brought for it a parallel: \"and the stock which your right hand planted, and upon the בן (branch) you made firm for yourself\" (Psalms 80:16). And the meaning is as though he said: a sapling on which are many פארות (branches) is Joseph.\n\nAnd in my opinion, \"בן\" is according to its plain meaning — he says that Joseph is a son who is like the פארות (branches) of a tree planted by a spring whose waters never fail, and its daughters march upon the high walls in the sky, and he called the shoots that emerge from the branch \"daughters,\" for they are daughters to the great branches. And this is an expression of eloquence for בן פורת. And בן is not joined to פורת, but it is like \"אשור ארז בלבנון\" (Assyria was a cedar in Lebanon, Ezekiel 31:3), \"נפתלי אילה שלוחה\" (Naphtali is a deer let loose, Genesis 49:21), \"בנימן זאב\" (Benjamin is a wolf, Genesis 49:27). And therefore it is pointed with a tzeirei, for if its meaning were a sapling that has branches, its proper vocalization would be with a segol. And he said \"בן\" as an expression of endearment, like \"מטרף בני עלית\" (Genesis 49:9).\n\nAnd in general, it is fitting that in the blessing of Joseph there be a remembrance of the two tribes that emerge from him, and this is the meaning of \"פורת\" and \"בנות\" (daughters). But since he has mentioned Levi, and the tribes of Israel are no more than twelve, he does not make of him in his blessing two separate tribes, but he alludes to them. And so with Moses our master, in his blessing, he likened him to a bull and to a re'em (Deuteronomy 33:17), and mentioned in it the horns, for each one of them is a single body from which horns branch out. And because there he did not mention Simeon, he stated explicitly \"and they are the tens of thousands of Ephraim and they are the thousands of Manasseh\" (Deuteronomy 33:17)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:25.816153545Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}